          "text": "{\"relation\":\"REFINED\",\"consensus_summary\":\"Heaven is primarily the transcendent sphere of God's presence, throne, and rule, and derivatively the blessed abode or destiny of the redeemed; it can also denote the visible sky, while final salvation may involve heaven and earth being united.\",\"book_summary\":\"Heaven is the jurisdiction in which God's will is already accomplished, encompassing the visible sky and the divine seat of rule; salvation proceeds from heaven downward to earth.\",\"consensus_core_identification\":\"The transcendent realm of God's presence and sovereign rule.\",\"book_core_identification\":\"The divine jurisdiction or sphere of God's effective rule.\",\"core_relation\":\"EQUIVALENT\",\"extension_relation\":\"PARTIAL_RECLASSIFICATION\",\"boundary_disagreement\":\"The book incorporates the visible sky into its identification and foregrounds heaven as the source from which salvation descends, rather than identifying it as humanity's final destination. It therefore reclassifies cases commonly described as the redeemed 'going to heaven,' while remaining compatible with an intermediate heavenly state and renewed-creation theology.\",\"material_contradiction\":\"There is no contradiction at the central referent: both identify heaven with God's sphere of rule. The material difference concerns eschatological direction and scope—the consensus often includes heaven as the redeemed's ultimate home, whereas the book emphasizes God's heavenly order coming down to earth.\",\"rationale\":\"The book preserves the consensus core of heaven as God's sovereign realm but sharpens it into a jurisdiction defined by the accomplished divine will. Its downward-movement framework changes the treatment of heaven as a final human destination without replacing the central divine-realm referent, making the relationship refined rather than divergent. The visible-sky and renewed-earth elements are already recognizable within the consensus alternatives.\",\"citation_support\":\"STRONG\",\"strongest_objection\":\"A MATCH classification is plausible because the consensus expressly includes divine rule, the visible sky, and the New Jerusalem's descent to renewed earth; the book may simply select and combine these established senses rather than materially revise them.\",\"confidence\":\"high\"}"
        }
